<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Al-Burda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as قصيدة البردة, Qasīdat al-Burda</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Qasīdat al-Burda, the &quot;Ode of the Mantle&quot;, is a thirteenth-century ode in praise of Muhammad by al-Busiri, a mystic of the Shadhili <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> order in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Its own title is &quot;The Celestial Lights in Praise of the Best of Creation&quot;.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The poem takes its name from a story told of its author. Al-Busiri, afflicted by illness, is said to have praised Muhammad without ceasing until Muhammad appeared to him in a dream and wrapped him in a mantle; in the morning the poet found that God had cured him. The name had an older history. Bānat Suʿād, a poem by Ka'b ibn Zuhayr, was first called Al-Burda: Ka'b recited it before Muhammad after embracing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, and Muhammad, moved, took off his mantle and wrapped it round him. That earlier Burda is less widely known than al-Busiri's.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The Burda has 160 verses in ten chapters, all on one rhyme: every verse ends in the Arabic letter mīm, a form called mīmiyya. Between the verses runs a refrain asking God to send blessings and peace continuously and eternally on His Beloved, the Best of All Creation. The chapters treat, in turn, lyrical love and yearning, warnings about the caprices of the self, the praise of the Prophet, his birth, his miracles, the exalted stature of the Qur'an, the Ascension, the struggle of God's Messenger, seeking intercession through the Prophet, and finally the poet's own intimate petition.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufis</a> have long venerated the poem. It is memorised and recited in congregations, above all on occasions such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mawlid">Mawlid</a>, and its verses decorate public buildings and mosques; for centuries it adorned the Prophet's Mosque in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, where all but two lines were later erased. More than 90 commentaries have been written on it, among them works by Ibn Hajar al-Haytami, Nazifi and Qastallani, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shafii-school">Shafi'i</a> hadith scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-hajar-al-asqalani">Ibn Hajar al-Asqalani</a> studied it through a chain of transmission going back to al-Busiri himself.</p><p>It has been translated into Persian, Turkish, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a>, Punjabi, Sindhi, Saraiki, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berber-languages">Berber languages</a>, French, German, Norwegian, Chinese and other languages, and into English by Timothy Winter. Not all scholars accepted it: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-abd-al-wahhab">Muhammad ibn Abd al-Wahhab</a> considered it shirk, or idolatry. The poem gave its name to a whole family of later works, from Ahmad Shawqi's Nahj al-Burda to Tamim al-Barghouti's Burdat Tamim, written in November and December 2010 and first published in 2013.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Al-Burda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
